Why should it be the same with e-mail?  There is nothing private about e-mail; 
the text of your message can be lying around in any number of places, and to 
the best of my knowledge there isn't really much decent case law on the 
subject yet.  Surely, we have all experienced receiving an automated 
"postmaster" reply to a message that didn't get through--with the WHOLE TEXT 
of the message repeated back to us. Our own sysops obviously have access to 
our messages, as do all kinds of folks along the way.

 While in most cases most people who could be monitoring these messages 
probably have better things to do with their time, I'm not sure there should 
be a presumption that there is anything like "privacy" involved in e-mail.   
If I'm sending a message--say, this message, for instance--to a newsgroup or a 
public list, I think it would behoove me to assume it was public property.  
The analogy would be to sending a letter to the newspaper editor rather than 
to making a telephone call.  But even in the case of an e-mail to a specific 
person's e-mail address, if it is going out over the internet, absolutely 
anyone might have access to it.  So I'm not sure that Guelph is out of line 
even if they do have a copy of every e-mail stored away somewhere.  
Anal-retentive, perhaps, but not out of line.

Anyway, I'd be interested to know how other people view this.

Jean Pfleiderer




From caf-talk Caf Aug 20 20:08:17 1994
From: dougj@pallas (Doug Johnstone)
Newsgroups: soc.culture.canada,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: E-mail and privacy (was Re: Proof Positive: U. of Guelph ...)
Date: 17 Aug 1994 20:59:06 GMT
Message-ID: <32ttmq$dh6@agate.berkeley.edu>

pfleiderer_j@wizard.colorado.edu writes:
> dougj@pallas (Doug Johnstone) writes:
>>My university is allowed to know when and where I place phone calls, but 
>>is not allowed to tap into the text of the conversation, I would have 
>>thought it the same when it comes to e-mail.
>
>Why should it be the same with e-mail?  There is nothing private about e-mail; 
>the text of your message can be lying around in any number of places, and to 
>the best of my knowledge there isn't really much decent case law on the 
>subject yet.  Surely, we have all experienced receiving an automated 
>"postmaster" reply to a message that didn't get through--with the WHOLE TEXT 
>of the message repeated back to us. Our own sysops obviously have access to 
>our messages, as do all kinds of folks along the way.
>
> While in most cases most people who could be monitoring these messages 
>probably have better things to do with their time, I'm not sure there should 
>be a presumption that there is anything like "privacy" involved in e-mail.   
>If I'm sending a message--say, this message, for instance--to a newsgroup or a 
>public list, I think it would behoove me to assume it was public property.  
>The analogy would be to sending a letter to the newspaper editor rather than 
>to making a telephone call.  But even in the case of an e-mail to a specific 
>person's e-mail address, if it is going out over the internet, absolutely 
>anyone might have access to it.  So I'm not sure that Guelph is out of line 
>even if they do have a copy of every e-mail stored away somewhere.  
>Anal-retentive, perhaps, but not out of line.

I am sure there are more knowledgeable people than me who will soon join this
quoting chapter and verse, but let me respond to your response.

I quite agree with just about everything you said.  Certainly posts to 
newsgroups are public once they are posted and privacy cannot be expected by
any but the foolish.  As for e-mail between two individuals, it is definitely 
true that at present these messages are not secure and as such one could 
likely argue that they are public domain.  If I were trying to send messages
privately across the internet I would be wise to use encryption techniques.
However, although it is true that sysops (postmasters) and others may have
access to my personal e-mail, I would consider it rude for them to use this
information other than in their capacity as systems op (ie to help send it to
the right location or fix a problem in the system).  Likewise I would consider
it rude for Guelph to keep the whole text of every e-mail I sent unless I was
warned about it ahead of time.  Is it against the law, I don't know.  Is it
proper functioning, I would feel uncomfortable knowing that it had been going
on without my knowledge.

My original post was not meant to pass judgement one way or the other but 
rather to ask for a clarification.  I have absolutely no problem with Guelph
keeping logs of e-mail destinations, but feel less sure about them storing
the text of these messages without consent and understanding (which they might
have since I am not at Guelph and can't say what published policy there is).

		doug johnstone (who still would like to know the answer to his
				question)

